\section{Parallel build}{

\describe{
\item{\code{tabxplor.parallel}}{\code{FALSE} (default): build the independent units of one call on parallel CPU cores (needs the \code{mirai} package): the per-\code{row_var} tables of a \code{\link[=tab]{tab()}}, the models / \code{tab_vars} groups / outcomes of a \code{\link[=tab_reg]{tab_reg()}}. The result is byte-identical to the serial one.  \code{FALSE} (default) never dispatches. \code{"auto"} (or \code{TRUE}) takes \strong{half the cores this session may actually use, at least 2 and at most 4} --- so 2 on a dual-core laptop, 2 on a 4-core machine, 4 on 8 cores or more. An integer takes that many verbatim. The count respects \code{options(mc.cores)}, a container's CPU quota and an HPC allocation (it reads them through the \code{parallelly} package when installed), and never exceeds 2 under \verb{R CMD check}.  It stays OPT-IN because starting the pool BLOCKS for about a second, so the first parallel table of a session is always slower than the serial one; from the third it is ahead. It pays off for MANY evenly sized units against a small or medium data frame --- 24 tables run about 2.8x faster on 4 workers --- and is a loss for few units or multi-million-row data, where shipping the population to each worker eats the gain. Set it once at the top of a script: \code{options(tabxplor.parallel = "auto")}. A model comparison (\code{stats = "compare_*"}) is always serial and says so when asked: it is a test BETWEEN the fits, so they are built together. For one call only, wrap it in \code{withr::with_options(list(tabxplor.parallel = "auto"), ...)}. The pool persists for the session; release it with \code{\link[=tab_parallel_stop]{tab_parallel_stop()}}.}
\item{\code{tabxplor.parallel_min}}{\code{2L} (default): the smallest UNIT count worth dispatching -- \code{row_var}s for \code{\link[=tab]{tab()}}, models for \code{\link[=tab_reg]{tab_reg()}} (fewer runs serially, since the setup would outweigh the gain).}
}
